          "session_id": "full-g-gh-020",
          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is described about salt and fluid for someone living with heart failure?",
          "answer": "**Short answer:** The sources I could search describe salt and fluid limits for heart failure as individualized. They don't give a universal number, and they say the evidence for strict limits is less settled than once assumed.\n\n**Salt (sodium)**\n- Sodium matters in heart failure mainly because it holds water in the body. Excess fluid can back up into the lungs and legs, causing breathlessness and swelling. This is different from blood pressure, where salt is restricted to lower a number. (Source: *Diet and fluid in heart failure: sodium, volume, and the daily weight*.)\n- Most dietary salt comes from packaged and restaurant food, not the salt shaker. Reading labels for sodium per serving is described as more effective than any single swap. (Same source, and *Diet for high blood pressure*.)\n- The source says the commonly stated targets are \"in a similar range\" to blood pressure targets, but it does not give a specific figure for heart failure. I can't give you one from these sources. Ask your own clinician what limit applies to you.\n- Guidance says very strict limits can reduce appetite and nutrition, especially in people already eating poorly. The evidence for aggressive restriction is described as less settled than it was once assumed. (*Diet and fluid in heart failure*.)\n\n**Fluid**\n- Fluid restriction is not routine. It is generally reserved for people with more advanced disease, persistent congestion despite treatment, or low blood sodium. Where it applies, the limit is set individually. (*Diet and fluid in heart failure*.)\n- Restricting fluid when it has not been advised can cause problems, particularly in hot weather, during illness with vomiting or diarrhea, or after a change in diuretic (water-pill) dose. (Same source.)\n\n**Where authorities disagree or evidence is thin**\n- The sources I searched did not report a disagreement between guidelines on salt or fluid limits in heart failure. They also did not return trial results on outcomes, so I can't tell you how strong the evidence is for specific limits. The evidence is described only as \"less settled.\"\n\n**Seek urgent care** if you have breathlessness at rest or that wakes you at night and does not ease when upright, chest pain, rapid weight gain over a few days, fainting, or confusion. Pink frothy spit with severe breathlessness is an emergency. Call emergency services now.\n\nYour daily weight, your fluid and salt targets, and any changes to them are best set with your heart care team.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
